Kernel-side ELF64 selftest — locks down the parser before execve
================================================================

Phase 1 chunk 7. Builds a tiny two-LOAD-segment ELF in a 256-byte
BSS buffer, runs it through every `oskit.lib.elf` accessor, and
returns 0 on pass or 1..N on the first failing step. There is no
userspace ELF producer wired up yet (sysl-built binaries are TRB
after objcopy), so this stand-in pins the parser's behavior —
and therefore chunk 8's `execve` and chunk 9's PT_INTERP handoff —
before any real ELF flows through the loader.

This file is the same `oskit.kernel` module as `kernel.lsysl`,
so `syscall_return` and `kernel_continue_current` resolve as
siblings without an import. The handler is registered into the
syscall dispatch table from kernel.lsysl's bootstrap with the
syscall number `SYS_ELF_SELFTEST = 92`.

    module oskit.kernel

    import oskit.lib.{ELF_OK, ELF_PT_LOAD, ELF_PF_R, ELF_PF_W, ELF_PF_X, ELF_ERR_NOT_ELF, ELF_ERR_TRUNCATED, elf64_validate, elf64_entry, elf64_phnum, elf64_ph_type, elf64_ph_vaddr, elf64_ph_memsz, elf64_ph_filesz, elf64_ph_flags, elf64_pf_to_vma_prot}


Phase 1 chunk 7 — synthetic ELF64 selftest. The kernel-side
`sys_elf_selftest_handler` (SYS_ELF_SELFTEST = 92) builds a tiny
two-LOAD-segment ELF in a 256-byte BSS buffer, runs it through
`oskit.lib.elf` accessors, and compares each field to a
hardcoded expected value. Returns 0 on pass, 1..N for the
failing step. There is no userspace ELF-file producer wired up
yet (sysl-built binaries are TRB after objcopy), so this stand-
in exists to lock down the parser's behavior — and therefore
chunk 8's `execve` and chunk 9's PT_INTERP handoff — before any
real ELF flows through the loader.

Little-endian field writers used only by the selftest. Kept
local to the kernel module so `oskit.lib.elf` stays a read-only
parser; if a future user ever needs to *produce* ELF in-kernel
the writers here can move out, but until then they're dead code
to everyone except the selftest.

    elf_write_u16_le(p: *byte, v: int)
        p[0] = byte(v & 0xFF)
        p[1] = byte((v >> 8) & 0xFF)

    elf_write_u32_le(p: *byte, v: i64)
        p[0] = byte(int(v) & 0xFF)
        p[1] = byte(int(v >> 8) & 0xFF)
        p[2] = byte(int(v >> 16) & 0xFF)
        p[3] = byte(int(v >> 24) & 0xFF)

    elf_write_u64_le(p: *byte, v: i64)
        p[0] = byte(int(v) & 0xFF)
        p[1] = byte(int(v >> 8) & 0xFF)
        p[2] = byte(int(v >> 16) & 0xFF)
        p[3] = byte(int(v >> 24) & 0xFF)
        p[4] = byte(int(v >> 32) & 0xFF)
        p[5] = byte(int(v >> 40) & 0xFF)
        p[6] = byte(int(v >> 48) & 0xFF)
        p[7] = byte(int(v >> 56) & 0xFF)

`elf64_selftest` returns 0 on pass, 1..N on the failing step. The
synthetic image describes two LOAD segments laid out the way a
real ELF would: a R+X text segment at 0x60001000 (memsz > filesz
so it has a tail of "padding" but no .bss because text is fully
initialized) and a R+W data segment at 0x60010000 with a 0x1000-
byte .bss tail. Step numbers are deliberately stable across the
positive and negative cases so a regression in any one accessor
is greppable from the test output.

    elf64_selftest() -> int
        var buf: [256]byte
        var i = 0
        while i < 256
            buf[i] = byte(0)
            i += 1

        buf[0] = 0x7F
        buf[1] = byte('E')
        buf[2] = byte('L')
        buf[3] = byte('F')
        buf[4] = byte(2)
        buf[5] = byte(1)

        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[24], i64(0x60001000))
        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[32], i64(64))
        elf_write_u16_le(&buf[54], 56)
        elf_write_u16_le(&buf[56], 2)

        val ph0 = 64
        elf_write_u32_le(&buf[ph0 + 0],  i64(ELF_PT_LOAD))
        elf_write_u32_le(&buf[ph0 + 4],  i64(ELF_PF_R | ELF_PF_X))
        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[ph0 + 8],  i64(0x100))
        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[ph0 + 16], i64(0x60001000))
        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[ph0 + 32], i64(0x1500))
        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[ph0 + 40], i64(0x2000))

        val ph1 = 120
        elf_write_u32_le(&buf[ph1 + 0],  i64(ELF_PT_LOAD))
        elf_write_u32_le(&buf[ph1 + 4],  i64(ELF_PF_R | ELF_PF_W))
        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[ph1 + 8],  i64(0x2000))
        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[ph1 + 16], i64(0x60010000))
        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[ph1 + 32], i64(0x2000))
        elf_write_u64_le(&buf[ph1 + 40], i64(0x3000))

        val pbuf: *byte = &buf[0]
        val total_len = ph1 + 56

        if elf64_validate(pbuf, total_len) != ELF_OK
            return 1
        if elf64_entry(pbuf) != u64(0x60001000)
            return 2
        if elf64_phnum(pbuf) != 2
            return 3

        if elf64_ph_type(pbuf, 0) != ELF_PT_LOAD
            return 4
        if elf64_ph_vaddr(pbuf, 0) != u64(0x60001000)
            return 5
        if elf64_ph_filesz(pbuf, 0) != i64(0x1500)
            return 6
        if elf64_ph_memsz(pbuf, 0) != i64(0x2000)
            return 7
        if elf64_pf_to_vma_prot(elf64_ph_flags(pbuf, 0)) != 5
            return 8

        if elf64_ph_type(pbuf, 1) != ELF_PT_LOAD
            return 9
        if elf64_ph_vaddr(pbuf, 1) != u64(0x60010000)
            return 10
        if elf64_ph_filesz(pbuf, 1) != i64(0x2000)
            return 11
        if elf64_ph_memsz(pbuf, 1) != i64(0x3000)
            return 12
        if elf64_pf_to_vma_prot(elf64_ph_flags(pbuf, 1)) != 3
            return 13

Negative-path tests. Mutate the buffer to break each invariant
in turn and verify `elf64_validate` returns the right error
code, then restore. The buffer is restored between mutations so
each negative case stands on its own.

        buf[0] = byte(0xFF)
        if elf64_validate(pbuf, total_len) != ELF_ERR_NOT_ELF
            return 14
        buf[0] = 0x7F

        if elf64_validate(pbuf, 32) != ELF_ERR_TRUNCATED
            return 15

        0

SYS_ELF_SELFTEST (92): runs the kernel-side ELF parser selftest
and returns its 0/N result. Debug-only — exists so the chunk-7
test program can validate `oskit.lib.elf` end-to-end without
yet having a real ELF binary in the ramdisk (that capability
arrives in chunk 8 with `exec()`).

    sys_elf_selftest_handler(arg: int)
        val rc = elf64_selftest()
        syscall_return(i64(rc))
        kernel_continue_current()

